{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\": \"R-46:21\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The command to remember ʿAd as a people who were warned makes their ruins in 29:38 part of a history of warning and response.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟ وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"46:21\", \"ar\": \"وَٱذْكُرْ أَخَا عَادٍ إِذْ أَنذَرَ قَوْمَهُۥ بِٱلْأَحْقَافِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared name ʿĀd connects the visible dwellings to a prophet’s warning to that people.\", \"limits\": \"46:21 speaks of ʿĀd alone and does not mention the dwellings or Thamud.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-46:24\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"ʿĀd see an approaching cloud and call it rain, but it is the punishment they had demanded; this is a concrete case of seeing while misreading, beside 29:38's claim that they were مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"46:24\", \"ar\": \"فَلَمَّا رَأَوْهُ عَارِضًۭا مُّسْتَقْبِلَ أَوْدِيَتِهِمْ قَالُوا۟ هَٰذَا عَارِضٌۭ مُّمْطِرُنَا ۚ بَلْ هُوَ مَا ٱسْتَعْجَلْتُم بِهِۦ\"}], \"activation\": \"The people named as ʿĀd in the focus are shown perceiving a visible sign but assigning it the wrong meaning.\", \"limits\": \"46:24 does not say that Satan caused this misreading or equate it with the focus’s beautification of deeds.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-46:25\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The shared word مَسَٰكِن links 29:38's evidence from dwellings to the aftermath of ʿĀd’s destruction, when only their dwellings could be seen.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"46:25\", \"ar\": \"فَأَصْبَحُوا۟ لَا يُرَىٰٓ إِلَّا مَسَٰكِنُهُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus explicitly makes the dwellings a source of evidence, and 46:25 describes them as the surviving visible trace of ʿĀd.\", \"limits\": \"46:25 describes ʿĀd; it does not establish that the same aftermath applied to Thamud.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-46:26\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The account of ʿĀd says that hearing, sight, and hearts did not benefit them because they denied God’s signs; this clarifies how having insight need not lead to a right response.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"46:26\", \"ar\": \"وَجَعَلْنَا لَهُمْ سَمْعًۭا وَأَبْصَٰرًۭا وَأَفْـِٔدَةًۭ فَمَآ أَغْنَىٰ عَنْهُمْ سَمْعُهُمْ وَلَآ أَبْصَٰرُهُمْ وَلَآ أَفْـِٔدَتُهُم مِّن شَىْءٍ إِذْ كَانُوا۟ يَجْحَدُونَ بِـَٔايَٰتِ ٱللَّهِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages concern ʿĀd and join perception with failure to heed signs.\", \"limits\": \"46:26 does not use مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ or explain the focus’s claim about Satan’s role.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-46:27\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus’s visible dwellings sit within a wider picture of destroyed settlements around the audience, whose signs were repeatedly shown in hope that people would return.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"46:27\", \"ar\": \"وَلَقَدْ أَهْلَكْنَا مَا حَوْلَكُم مِّنَ ٱلْقُرَىٰ وَصَرَّفْنَا ٱلْءَايَٰتِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَرْجِعُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages address people through evidence of earlier destruction.\", \"limits\": \"46:27 does not name ʿĀd or Thamud or specify that the settlements’ dwellings remain visible.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:1\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The pairing of blocking the way with an adverse outcome for the blockers’ deeds parallels the focus’s sequence from أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ to obstruction from ٱلسَّبِيلِ.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:1\", \"ar\": \"ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ أَضَلَّ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages place أعمال beside صَدُّوا عن سبيل الله.\", \"limits\": \"47:1 does not describe beautification or identify its people as ʿĀd or Thamud.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:8\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus links deeds to obstruction; 47:8–9 gives a related outcome in which the deeds of disbelievers are brought to nothing after their rejection.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:8\", \"ar\": \"وَٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ فَتَعْسًۭا لَّهُمْ وَأَضَلَّ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:9\", \"ar\": \"ذَٰلِكَ بِأَنَّهُمْ كَرِهُوا۟ مَآ أَنزَلَ ٱللَّهُ فَأَحْبَطَ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared أعمال term connects practices to the consequences of rejection.\", \"limits\": \"47:8–9 says nothing about Satan, beautification, or obstruction from the path.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:10\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The call to travel and see the fate of earlier peoples complements the focus’s presentation of dwellings as evidence for an addressed audience.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:10\", \"ar\": \"أَفَلَمْ يَسِيرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ فَيَنظُرُوا۟ كَيْفَ كَانَ عَٰقِبَةُ ٱلَّذِينَ مِن قَبْلِهِمْ ۚ دَمَّرَ ٱللَّهُ عَلَيْهِمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages direct attention to observable evidence of earlier destruction.\", \"limits\": \"47:10 does not identify the peoples or say that their dwellings are the evidence being viewed.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:14\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The contrast between one on clear proof and one whose bad deed is beautified and who follows desire closely parallels the focus’s juxtaposition of insight with Satan’s beautification of deeds.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:14\", \"ar\": \"أَفَمَن كَانَ عَلَىٰ بَيِّنَةٍۢ مِّن رَّبِّهِۦ كَمَن زُيِّنَ لَهُۥ سُوٓءُ عَمَلِهِۦ وَٱتَّبَعُوٓا۟ أَهْوَآءَهُم\"}], \"activation\": \"The same beautification verb زُيِّنَ appears alongside clear proof, making the focus’s pairing of perception and deception especially legible.\", \"limits\": \"47:14 does not name Satan or connect its people to ʿĀd and Thamud.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:23\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus presents ʿĀd and Thamud as مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ, while 47:23 depicts people whose sight is blinded; the contrast raises the difference between misused discernment and lost capacity.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:23\", \"ar\": \"أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ لَعَنَهُمُ ٱللَّهُ فَأَصَمَّهُمْ وَأَعْمَىٰٓ أَبْصَٰرَهُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both statements describe the relation between perception and response to guidance.\", \"limits\": \"47:23 concerns different people and does not explain the focus’s combination of insight and diversion.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:25\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The sequence “after guidance became clear” followed by Satan’s prompting closely parallels the focus’s “it became clear to you” and Satan’s beautifying deeds before turning the people from the way.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ ۖ وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:25\", \"ar\": \"مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلْهُدَى ۙ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ سَوَّلَ لَهُمْ وَأَمْلَىٰ لَهُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated تبين, Satan, and لَهُم sequence links clear evidence with subsequent temptation.\", \"limits\": \"47:25 describes another group and uses سَوَّلَ rather than زَيَّنَ.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:32\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"This verse explicitly joins obstruction from God’s way with rejection after guidance became clear, giving a close parallel for the focus’s diverted people who had been مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:32\", \"ar\": \"وَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ وَشَآقُّوا۟ ٱلرَّسُولَ مِنۢ بَعْدِ مَا تَبَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلْهُدَىٰ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages put path obstruction beside clear prior evidence or guidance.\", \"limits\": \"47:32 does not mention Satan or the dwellings of earlier peoples.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-47:34\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The repeated formula كَفَرُوا وَصَدُّوا عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ adds the outcome of dying in disbelief and not being forgiven to the focus’s account of obstruction.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"47:34\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا۟ وَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ ثُمَّ مَاتُوا۟ وَهُمْ كُفَّارٌۭ فَلَن يَغْفِرَ ٱللَّهُ لَهُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"The same path-obstruction formula links the two accounts.\", \"limits\": \"47:34 says nothing about beautified deeds or prior insight.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-48:12\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus names Satan as beautifier of deeds; 48:12 uses the same beautification verb for a bad expectation settled in people’s hearts, suggesting that the Qur’anic image can describe inner framing as well as attractive actions.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"48:12\", \"ar\": \"وَزُيِّنَ ذَٰلِكَ فِى قُلُوبِكُمْ وَظَنَنتُمْ ظَنَّ ٱلسَّوْءِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated زَيَّنَ form activates a comparison between beautified deeds and beautified suspicion.\", \"limits\": \"48:12 is passive and does not identify Satan as the agent; its object is bad suspicion, not deeds.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-49:18\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-50:12\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Thamud appears among earlier peoples who denied, placing one of the focus’s named groups within a broader catalogue of rejection.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"50:12\", \"ar\": \"كَذَّبَتْ قَبْلَهُمْ قَوْمُ نُوحٍۢ وَأَصْحَٰبُ ٱلرَّسِّ وَثَمُودُ\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated name Thamud connects the focus to a catalogue of earlier deniers.\", \"limits\": \"50:12 does not mention dwellings, insight, or Satanic influence.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-50:13\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The next verse names ʿĀd after 50:12 has named Thamud, placing both focus peoples within the same wider list of past deniers.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"50:12\", \"ar\": \"كَذَّبَتْ قَبْلَهُمْ قَوْمُ نُوحٍۢ وَأَصْحَٰبُ ٱلرَّسِّ وَثَمُودُ\"}, {\"ref\": \"50:13\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادٌۭ وَفِرْعَوْنُ وَإِخْوَٰنُ لُوطٍۢ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s two names recur in consecutive verses of a larger catalogue.\", \"limits\": \"The catalogue does not pair ʿĀd and Thamud alone or describe their dwellings or insight.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-50:27\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The companion’s denial that he made a person transgress, alongside the person’s distant error, may offer a scene of disputed agency relevant to the focus’s attribution of diversion to Satan.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"50:27\", \"ar\": \"قَالَ قَرِينُهُۥ رَبَّنَا مَآ أَطْغَيْتُهُۥ وَلَٰكِن كَانَ فِى ضَلَٰلٍۭ بَعِيدٍۢ\"}], \"missing\": \"The passage does not identify the قَرِين as Satan or connect his denial to beautifying deeds; a Quranic usage parallel or context identifying this companion could decide the link.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-50:36\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The destruction of powerful earlier generations is presented as a reminder for a receptive heart or attentive listener, complementing the focus’s evidence from surviving dwellings.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"50:36\", \"ar\": \"وَكَمْ أَهْلَكْنَا قَبْلَهُم مِّن قَرْنٍ هُمْ أَشَدُّ مِنْهُم بَطْشًۭا فَنَقَّبُوا۟ فِى ٱلْبِلَٰدِ هَلْ مِن مَّحِيصٍ\"}, {\"ref\": \"50:37\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَذِكْرَىٰ لِمَن كَانَ لَهُۥ قَلْبٌ أَوْ أَلْقَى ٱلسَّمْعَ وَهُوَ شَهِيدٌۭ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages treat the fate of earlier peoples as evidence whose lesson depends on the audience’s attention.\", \"limits\": \"50:36–37 does not identify those peoples as ʿĀd or Thamud or mention surviving dwellings.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-51:20\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus’s visible dwellings exemplify the broader statement that signs are present on earth for those who are certain.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:20\", \"ar\": \"وَفِى ٱلْأَرْضِ ءَايَٰتٌۭ لِّلْمُوقِنِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages locate evidence in the earth and address how people receive it.\", \"limits\": \"51:20 does not identify former dwellings as the signs in view or mention ʿĀd and Thamud.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-51:21\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The question أَفَلَا تُبْصِرُونَ makes perception an expected response to signs within oneself, beside the focus’s paradox that a people could be مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ yet diverted.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:21\", \"ar\": \"وَفِىٓ أَنفُسِكُمْ ۚ أَفَلَا تُبْصِرُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages use the b-ṣ-r root in relation to recognizing signs.\", \"limits\": \"51:21 concerns signs within people and does not describe the focus’s historical communities or their diversion.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-51:37\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"After Lot’s people are destroyed, a sign is left for those who fear punishment; this parallels the focus’s surviving dwellings as readable traces of judgment.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:37\", \"ar\": \"وَتَرَكْنَا فِيهَآ ءَايَةًۭ لِّلَّذِينَ يَخَافُونَ ٱلْعَذَابَ ٱلْأَلِيمَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages connect the aftermath of a destroyed community with evidence available to later observers.\", \"limits\": \"51:37 does not say that the remaining sign was a dwelling or identify its audience with the focus’s addressees.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-51:41\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The passage gives a further account of ʿĀd’s destruction by a barren wind, with the next verse depicting what the wind did to whatever it struck.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:41\", \"ar\": \"وَفِى عَادٍ إِذْ أَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِمُ ٱلرِّيحَ ٱلْعَقِيمَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:42\", \"ar\": \"مَا تَذَرُ مِن شَىْءٍ أَتَتْ عَلَيْهِ إِلَّا جَعَلَتْهُ كَٱلرَّمِيمِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared name ʿĀd supplies another account of the people named in the focus.\", \"limits\": \"This passage does not say that the destroyed dwellings were the evidence seen by the focus’s audience.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-51:43\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Thamud are told to enjoy themselves only until a set time; the following verse says they defied the command and were seized while looking, joining warning, defiance, and perception.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:43\", \"ar\": \"وَفِى ثَمُودَ إِذْ قِيلَ لَهُمْ تَمَتَّعُوا۟ حَتَّىٰ حِينٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:44\", \"ar\": \"فَعَتَوْا۟ عَنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّهِمْ فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلصَّٰعِقَةُ وَهُمْ يَنظُرُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus names Thamud and describes them as discerning; this passage shows their punishment arriving while they look after a warning.\", \"limits\": \"Looking at the punishment does not itself establish inner discernment or the focus’s Satanic mechanism.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-51:44\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Thamud are seized by the thunderbolt while they look, a sharp image of perception present at the moment of punishment that complicates the focus’s مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"51:44\", \"ar\": \"فَعَتَوْا۟ عَنْ أَمْرِ رَبِّهِمْ فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلصَّٰعِقَةُ وَهُمْ يَنظُرُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The same people are named in the focus and the related scene explicitly says they looked as judgment came.\", \"limits\": \"51:44 establishes visible punishment, not that they understood its meaning beforehand.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-52:15\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The question “is this magic, or do you not perceive?” turns failed recognition into an explicit issue at the sight of punishment, resonating with the focus’s claim that the punished people had been مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"52:15\", \"ar\": \"أَفَسِحْرٌ هَٰذَآ أَمْ أَنتُمْ لَا تُبْصِرُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages use the b-ṣ-r root to frame whether people truly perceive what is before them.\", \"limits\": \"52:15 addresses people facing the afterlife punishment and does not name ʿĀd or Thamud.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-53:17\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The statement that sight neither swerved nor overstepped offers a limited contrast with the focus’s discernment language and the people’s being turned from the way.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"53:17\", \"ar\": \"مَا زَاغَ ٱلْبَصَرُ وَمَا طَغَىٰ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages draw on the b-ṣ-r root, with one describing straight sight and the other discernment amid diversion.\", \"limits\": \"53:17 concerns a different scene and does not connect sight to a path or to ʿĀd and Thamud.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-53:50\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The passage names ʿĀd and then Thamud as consecutive destruction cases, repeating the focus’s pair in the same order within a wider account of earlier peoples.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"53:50\", \"ar\": \"وَأَنَّهُۥٓ أَهْلَكَ عَادًا ٱلْأُولَىٰ\"}, {\"ref\": \"53:51\", \"ar\": \"وَثَمُودَا۟ فَمَآ أَبْقَىٰ\"}], \"activation\": \"The exact pair recurs across adjacent verses in a retrospective account of destruction.\", \"limits\": \"This passage does not describe the dwellings as evidence or mention Satanic beautification.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-53:51\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The phrase فَمَآ أَبْقَىٰ after Thamud may invite a question about what was left, since 29:38 says their dwellings remain available as evidence.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"53:51\", \"ar\": \"وَثَمُودَا۟ فَمَآ أَبْقَىٰ\"}], \"missing\": \"The object of أَبْقَىٰ is not stated here; context or a parallel use could establish whether it means no people were spared or whether it also concerns material traces, deciding how this relates to the visible dwellings.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-54:18\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus names ʿĀd among visible precedents; this passage states directly that ʿĀd denied the warnings and asks the audience to consider the resulting punishment.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"54:18\", \"ar\": \"كَذَّبَتْ عَادٌۭ فَكَيْفَ كَانَ عَذَابِى وَنُذُرِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared name connects the focus’s people with an explicit account of their denial and punishment.\", \"limits\": \"54:18 does not mention surviving dwellings or the focus’s account of Satanic beautification.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-54:20\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The passage depicts ʿĀd’s punishment as people pulled out like uprooted palm trunks, adding a bodily image to the destruction associated with the focus’s named people.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا\"}, {\"ref\": \"54:20\", \"ar\": \"تَنزِعُ ٱلنَّاسَ كَأَنَّهُمْ أَعْجَازُ نَخْلٍۢ مُّنقَعِرٍۢ\"}], \"activation\": \"The surrounding passage identifies the people under punishment as ʿĀd.\", \"limits\": \"This image describes the people, not the dwellings that serve as evidence in the focus.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-54:23\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Thamud are explicitly said to have denied the warnings, supplying a response to warning that the focus’s brief naming does not state.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"54:23\", \"ar\": \"كَذَّبَتْ ثَمُودُ بِٱلنُّذُرِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The same people recur, here with denial of warnings made explicit.\", \"limits\": \"54:23 does not connect their denial to visible dwellings or beautified deeds.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-54:31\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The Thamud episode describes a single cry leaving the people like dry brush, a compact account of punishment for one of the focus’s named peoples.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"54:23\", \"ar\": \"كَذَّبَتْ ثَمُودُ بِٱلنُّذُرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"54:31\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّآ أَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِمْ صَيْحَةًۭ وَٰحِدَةًۭ فَكَانُوا۟ كَهَشِيمِ ٱلْمُحْتَظِرِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The surrounding Thamud passage identifies the people seized by the cry.\", \"limits\": \"The image adds the manner of their destruction but says nothing about dwellings or their prior insight.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-54:51\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The reminder to heed the destruction of earlier counterparts extends the focus’s historical warning into a broader appeal to remember past communities.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"54:51\", \"ar\": \"وَلَقَدْ أَهْلَكْنَآ أَشْيَاعَكُمْ فَهَلْ مِن مُّدَّكِرٍۢ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both place earlier destruction before an audience as a prompt to take heed.\", \"limits\": \"54:51 does not name ʿĀd or Thamud or specify visible remains.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-56:46\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-57:4\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-58:10\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The same named agent, Satan, is also linked to a different tactic: secret conversation that grieves believers.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"58:10\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّمَا ٱلنَّجْوَىٰ مِنَ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنِ لِيَحْزُنَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared agent opens a comparison between ways Satan affects people.\", \"limits\": \"58:10 does not mention beautification, deeds, or turning people from the way.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-58:19\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Satan’s domination and causing people to forget God’s remembrance gives another account of inner diversion alongside the focus’s beautification of deeds and turning from the way.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"58:19\", \"ar\": \"ٱسْتَحْوَذَ عَلَيْهِمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ فَأَنسَىٰهُمْ ذِكْرَ ٱللَّهِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages name Satan and describe an effect on a group through a causal sequence.\", \"limits\": \"58:19 describes forgetting remembrance, not beautification or obstruction from a path.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-59:2\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The people expelled from their homes wrongly trust their fortresses, then ruin their own houses; the command to take heed, “people of insight,” makes this a close parallel for dwellings as evidence and perception’s accountability.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَقَد تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"59:2\", \"ar\": \"وَظَنُّوٓا۟ أَنَّهُم مَّانِعَتُهُمْ حُصُونُهُم مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ فَأَتَىٰهُمُ ٱللَّهُ مِنْ حَيْثُ لَمْ يَحْتَسِبُوا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"59:2\", \"ar\": \"يُخْرِبُونَ بُيُوتَهُم بِأَيْدِيهِمْ وَأَيْدِى ٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ فَٱعْتَبِرُوا۟ يَٰٓأُو۟لِى ٱلْأَبْصَٰرِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages join homes, mistaken judgment, and an audience called to read the consequences.\", \"limits\": \"59:2 concerns a different community and does not attribute its mistaken confidence to Satan’s beautification.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-59:16\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"This parable shows Satan urging a person to disbelieve and then disavowing him, complementing the focus’s account of Satan making deeds attractive before their consequences unfold.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"59:16\", \"ar\": \"كَمَثَلِ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنِ إِذْ قَالَ لِلْإِنسَٰنِ ٱكْفُرْ فَلَمَّا كَفَرَ قَالَ إِنِّى بَرِىٓءٌۭ مِّنكَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages name Satan and portray his relation to human wrongdoing and its outcome.\", \"limits\": \"59:16 describes an invitation and disavowal, not the beautification of deeds or the historical peoples in the focus.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-60:1\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"This verse presents a different route to straying: misplaced affection for enemies ends in deviation from the right way, alongside the focus’s account of people turned from the way.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"60:1\", \"ar\": \"وَمَن يَفْعَلْهُ مِنكُمْ فَقَدْ ضَلَّ سَوَآءَ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages describe departure from a recognized way, with 60:1 tying it to a specific social allegiance.\", \"limits\": \"60:1 does not mention ʿĀd, Thamud, Satan, or beautification.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-63:2\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The exact formula صَدُّوا عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ recurs, here with oaths used as a shield; it supplies a social form of concealment beside the focus’s beautified deeds and obstruction.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"63:2\", \"ar\": \"ٱتَّخَذُوٓا۟ أَيْمَٰنَهُمْ جُنَّةًۭ فَصَدُّوا۟ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated صَدُّوا عن سبيل phrase connects the acts of obstruction; the shield image provides a distinct means.\", \"limits\": \"63:2 does not attribute obstruction to Satan or describe deeds being beautified.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-68:5\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The promise that the Prophet and his opponents will see, followed by a question about who is afflicted, makes contested perception a recurring issue beside the focus’s claim of insight.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"68:5\", \"ar\": \"فَسَتُبْصِرُ وَيُبْصِرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"68:6\", \"ar\": \"بِأَييِّكُمُ ٱلْمَفْتُونُ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages use the b-ṣ-r root where people’s sound judgment is at issue.\", \"limits\": \"68:5–6 promises future clarification in another setting and says nothing about ruined peoples or their dwellings.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-68:7\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The distinction between one who strays from God’s way and the guided offers a concise parallel to the focus’s claim that people were turned from the way despite their discernment.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَكَانُوا۟ مُسْتَبْصِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"68:7\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ رَبَّكَ هُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِمَن ضَلَّ عَن سَبِيلِهِۦ وَهُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِٱلْمُهْتَدِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages place straying and the path in view, while 68:7 adds the distinction of divine knowledge.\", \"limits\": \"68:7 does not describe beautification, historical remains, or prior insight.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-69:4\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Thamud and ʿĀd recur together, here explicitly as peoples who denied the overwhelming event; the paired names in the focus thus carry a shared history of rejection as well as visible aftermath.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"69:4\", \"ar\": \"كَذَّبَتْ ثَمُودُ وَعَادٌۢ بِٱلْقَارِعَةِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The two named groups recur together in reverse order and are explicitly joined by denial.\", \"limits\": \"69:4 does not mention their dwellings or explain Satan’s role in their conduct.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-69:5\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The next verses specify different fates for the pair: Thamud are destroyed by الطاغية, giving a compact account of the punishment behind the focus’s historical reference.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَثَمُودَا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"69:5\", \"ar\": \"فَأَمَّا ثَمُودُ فَأُهْلِكُوا۟ بِٱلطَّاغِيَةِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared name connects the focus’s Thamud with a specific account of destruction.\", \"limits\": \"69:5 does not explain the visible dwellings or their diversion from the way.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-69:6\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The account identifies a different punishment for ʿĀd: destruction by a violent wind.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَعَادًا\"}, {\"ref\": \"69:6\", \"ar\": \"وَأَمَّا عَادٌۭ فَأُهْلِكُوا۟ بِرِيحٍۢ صَرْصَرٍ عَاتِيَةٍۢ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s named people recur in a passage distinguishing the fates of ʿĀd and Thamud.\", \"limits\": \"69:6 adds the means of destruction but not the focus’s evidence-from-dwellings or insight theme.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-69:7\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The aftermath of ʿĀd is made visible through fallen bodies like hollow palm trunks, a bodily counterpart to the focus’s surviving dwellings as traces of destruction.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"69:6\", \"ar\": \"وَأَمَّا عَادٌۭ فَأُهْلِكُوا۟ بِرِيحٍۢ صَرْصَرٍ عَاتِيَةٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"69:7\", \"ar\": \"فَتَرَى ٱلْقَوْمَ فِيهَا صَرْعَىٰ كَأَنَّهُمْ أَعْجَازُ نَخْلٍ خَاوِيَةٍۢ\"}], \"activation\": \"The surrounding verses identify the people as ʿĀd, and 69:7 explicitly directs the audience to see the aftermath.\", \"limits\": \"69:7 describes fallen people, not surviving dwellings or evidence available to later generations.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-69:39\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The focus joins dwellings that have become clear to an audience with Satan as the agent behind the people’s deeds; the paired oath on what is seen and unseen may offer a frame for hearing these visible and hidden dimensions together.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"تَّبَيَّنَ لَكُم مِّن مَّسَٰكِنِهِمْ ۖ وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"69:38\", \"ar\": \"فَلَآ أُقْسِمُ بِمَا تُبْصِرُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"69:39\", \"ar\": \"وَمَا لَا تُبْصِرُونَ\"}], \"missing\": \"The verses do not identify what is seen or unseen as the focus’s dwellings and Satanic agency; a surrounding textual link or Quranic parallel assigning these referents could establish the connection.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-74:49\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The people turning away from the reminder offers a response parallel to being turned from the way in the focus.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"74:49\", \"ar\": \"فَمَا لَهُمْ عَنِ ٱلتَّذْكِرَةِ مُعْرِضِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both use an away-from construction to describe departure from guidance.\", \"limits\": \"74:49 does not mention Satan, prior insight, or historical evidence.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"low\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-79:11\", \"status\": \"none\", \"code\": \"no-link\"}\n{\"id\": \"X1\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The passage from Midian through the focus and on to Qarun, Pharaoh, and Haman builds a sequence of rejected calls and destruction; 29:40 then gathers the cases under the claim that each was seized for its sin. The focus’s beautification and obstruction is one distinct profile within that sequence.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:37\", \"ar\": \"فَكَذَّبُوهُ فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ ٱلرَّجْفَةُ فَأَصْبَحُوا۟ فِى دَارِهِمْ جَٰثِمِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"29:39\", \"ar\": \"وَلَقَدْ جَآءَهُم مُّوسَىٰ بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ فَٱسْتَكْبَرُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"29:40\", \"ar\": \"فَكُلًّا أَخَذْنَا بِذَنۢبِهِۦ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus is positioned between two accounts of rejection and followed by an inclusive summary of distinct punishments.\", \"limits\": \"29:40 links each group to its own sin; it does not say that all shared the focus’s specific Satanic mechanism.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"X2\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus gives a historical example of being turned from a path, beside the Fatiha’s repeated petition for guidance to the straight path and exclusion from the paths of error.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"1:6\", \"ar\": \"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"1:7\", \"ar\": \"غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus’s obstruction from ٱلسَّبِيلِ can be heard beside the worshippers’ request for guidance and avoidance of error.\", \"limits\": \"The Fatiha uses الصِّرَاط rather than السَّبِيل and does not identify ʿĀd or Thamud with any named group in its closing verse.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"X3\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Earlier in the same surah, disbelievers invite believers to follow their path and falsely offer to carry their sins; this gives a social example of an attractive but deceptive path beside the focus’s account of Satan beautifying deeds and diverting people.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"29:12\", \"ar\": \"ٱتَّبِعُوا۟ سَبِيلَنَا وَلْنَحْمِلْ خَطَٰيَٰكُمْ وَمَا هُم بِحَٰمِلِينَ مِنْ خَطَٰيَٰهُم مِّن شَىْءٍ ۖ إِنَّهُمْ لَكَٰذِبُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"29:38\", \"ar\": \"وَزَيَّنَ لَهُمُ ٱلشَّيْطَٰنُ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ فَصَدَّهُمْ عَنِ ٱلسَّبِيلِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both scenes involve a path offered to people and a deceptive influence that leads toward wrongdoing.\", \"limits\": \"29:12 concerns the surah’s contemporary disbelievers and does not identify their invitation as Satan’s work or equate them with ʿĀd and Thamud.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}"}}
